% c348: T's climax A (162, 178, 218, 350) is attacked on beat 3 and tied over the bar line (a syncopated anticipation); the crescendo now ends on that attack and the diminuendo starts on the next downbeat (A-S Unit 3 PDF 51, Ex. 3-6: Beethoven's sf on the syncopated attack; "the beginning of a note is heard as stronger than its continuation"). Also tA.ily, coda.ily.
% c318: 217-220 basses slurred in two-bar bows with the cellos: crescendo 217 on an up-bow, the f at 219 and its diminuendo on a down-bow (Adler PDF 38); 223 up-bow before the pizz. now natural.
% c257: 210 the forte return of T is no longer harmonised as 170 note for note: the bass rises E-G sharp (I6) under the held B, and the cellos and Bsn 2 exchange the voice (B2-G sharp 3 | E3-G sharp 3, were B2-E3 | G sharp 3; G sharp 3 returns on beat 3 so the cellos do not move E3-F sharp 3 in octaves with Bsn 1 and Hn 2 at 211), so 209-211 has a bass line E-G sharp-B into V instead of E-E-B (Sebesky PDF 19: avoid harmonising a melody the same way twice; PDF 20: a substitution for a little lift). Cb, Cbsn and harp LH take G sharp 2; Hn 2 holds E4 (G sharp 4 would make octaves with the new bass). 170 keeps the plain I.
% c218: 216 Vc b,4 its own bow and 222 b,2 its own bow, so the cellos' swell 217-218 is an up-bow, the f diminuendo at 219 a down-bow (with the violins, c118), and 223 keeps its up-bow before the pizz. (c096) without a hook; Adler PDF 38, 54.
% TRIO, third part, bars 209-240.
% c022: 225-236 the T head steps B-C sharp-B over the plagal IV (Ob 1, Cl 1, Bsn 1; Hn 3 A): I-IV-I with the 5-6-5 neighbour (A-S PDF 195-196).
% 209-224: T returns, forte espressivo: Vln I with Fl 1 at the octave above Cl 1; Vln II and violas off-beat; the cellos' countermelody;
%   harp; Hn 1-2 sustain the harmony (no unison horns: the high horn unison is kept for the finale).
% 225-240: codetta over the E pedal with plagal A-E: T's head falls through Ob 1 (225), Cl 1 (229), Bsn 1 (233; c003: Hn 1 keeps T for the coda, now at 341; c103 renumbered); poco rit. 237-240.

% c061: 221-222 T's cadence with the tied V4-3 suspension E-D sharp, as at 165-166 and 181-182 (see tA.ily; Aldwell-Schachter PDF 352 sec. 8).
% c069: Fl 1 rests 209-216 and enters at 217 for the third phrase's climb to A6 (Adler II PDF 37: hold a colour back for a specific event; PDF 156: a rest before a colour enters makes it clearly perceived; PDF 165: sustained doublings at pitch tire the ear and lose colour). Fl 2's G sharp at 209 (c050/c065) is kept.
% c093: 215-216 the raised 4th of V7/V (A sharp) only in the violas: cellos and Bsn 2 hold C sharp 3, Hn 1 holds C sharp 5 (was A sharp 4, an octave over the violas' A sharp 3,
%   both rising to B together at 216); Hn 1 still reaches B4 on beat 2 of 216, the breath and slurs unchanged (Aldwell-Schachter Unit 14, PDF 214: the raised 4th is not doubled).
% c098: 221-222 the cadential 4-3 shaded <> as at 165-166 (Aldwell-Schachter PDF 348, 350).
tCFluteOne = { R2.*8 | cis'''2(\mf\< dis'''4 | e'''4 gis'''4 a'''4~\f | a'''4.\> gis'''8 fis'''4 | cis'''4 b''4 a''4) | gis''4\mf( b''4 e'''4\<~ | e'''4-\tweak stencil ##f \f\> dis'''4-\tweak stencil ##f \mf fis'''4 | e'''2.~ | e'''2)\> r4\! | R2.*16 | }
% c158: 225-236 each codetta statement of T's head (Ob 1, Cl 1, Bsn 1) swells to its third bar (C sharp 6, C sharp 5, C natural 4) and falls back (hidden mp/p targets, c138): Sebesky Ex. 2A, Ex. 269 (PDF 18, 205): every line its own < > arc; PDF 207: carry one bar into the next.
tCOboeOne = { R2.*16 | gis''2(\p^\markup \italic "dolce" a''4 | b''2.\< | cis'''2.-\tweak stencil ##f \mp\> | b''2-\tweak stencil ##f \p) r4 | R2.*12 | }
% c367: 209-224 Cl 1 a level under Vln II, whose lower octave of T it doubles (mf, hidden mp/f peaks; was f/ff with the violins); Cl 2's lone G#4 at 209 removed (the end of the 205-208 line it gave to Hn 1 in c324). Sebesky PDF 164 (one woodwind weighs about sixteen violins; the wind is marked a level under the strings), PDF 138 rule 2 (the lead line carries more weight than any other line), PDF 145 (economy). Also coda 349-356 (ppp).
tCClOne = { gis'2(\mf^\markup \italic "espress." a'4 | b'2 e''4 | dis''4. fis'8 a'4~ | a'4\> gis'2-\tweak stencil ##f \mp) | \partCombineSoloI a'2-\tweak stencil ##f \mf( b'4 | cis''4\< fis''2-\tweak stencil ##f \f\> | e''4.-\tweak stencil ##f \mf dis''8 cis''4 | ais'4 b'2) \breathe | cis''2(\< dis''4 | e''4 gis''4 a''4~-\tweak stencil ##f \f | a''4.\> gis''8 fis''4 | cis''4 b'4 a'4) | gis'4-\tweak stencil ##f \mf( b'4 e''4\<~ | e''4-\tweak stencil ##f \f\> dis''4-\tweak stencil ##f \mf fis''4 | e''2.~ | e''2)\> r4\! | \partCombineAutomatic R2.*4 | gis'2(\p^\markup \italic "dolce" a'4 | b'2.\< | cis''2.-\tweak stencil ##f \mp\> | b'2-\tweak stencil ##f \p) r4 | R2.*8 | }  % c176: breath at the half-way cadence of T (bar 8 of the tune), where the horns and bassoons breathe; 16 bars of slurred melody otherwise (Sebesky PDF 24: a slur is played in one breath; PDF 70: give the player breathing spaces)  % c325: \sysLabel "1." at 214 (the system now starts at 214, not 213, so the combiner's "1." at 213 is on the previous system; Adler II PDF 295)
% c050: 209 Fl 2 and Cl 2 end the motto-tail line B-A sharp-A (206-208) on its G sharp, the first note of Fl 1 and Cl 1's T (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 125-126:
%   in a transferred phrase the last note of one part coincides with the first note of the next), so the quotation resolves in its own voice, as Hn 1's at 357-360. The G sharp is re-attacked with T's first note (no slur across: the part combiner merges the unison).
tCFluteTwo = { gis''2\mf r4 | R2.*31 | }
% c127: 229-232 Cl 2 rests and Hn 4 holds the E pedal (E3, pp) under Cl 1's statement of T's head: Cl 2's E4 lay a third under the clarinet solo in its own colour,
%   so Cl 1 entered as one of a pair while Ob 1 (225) and Bsn 1 (233, c126) state the head alone (Adler II PDF 267: Mozart writes no orchestral flutes beside the
%   solo flute and holds the tonic in the horns and basses; PDF 264: no orchestral horns beside the solo horn; PDF 274: registral distance). Cl 2 keeps 225-228 under the oboe.
% c288: 225-228 Cl 2's pp E4 under Ob 1's dolce solo slurred with tenuto, as Hn 4's pedal at 229-236 and Bsn 1 at 209-216 (Adler I PDF 309, 321).
tCClTwo = { R2. | R2.*3 | \partCombineSoloI R2.*12 | \partCombineAutomatic e'2.--(\pp | e'2.-- | e'2.--\> | e'2--) r4\! | R2.*4 | R2.*8 | }
% c023: Hn 1 holds G sharp 237-240 (moved from the retransition's 241-244), so the inflection G sharp -> G natural falls on 241, the first bar of the
%   retransition (A&S PDF 584-586, modulation by chromatic inflection); timpani retune G sharp -> G during the rests at 225.
% c042: 235 the third plagal of the codetta is iv (A minor, mixture), not IV: Bsn 1 B-C natural-B (flat 6 as upper neighbour to 5), harp C natural;
%   the change of mode varies the third statement and prepares the E minor of 241 (A-S PDF 387, 390 Ex. 23-5, 392-393 sec. 5).
% c014: 209-224 Bsn 1 an inner part between the bass and Hn 1-2 (2 bassoons + 2 horns in four parts, the 7ths in the bassoon; Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 100, 108)
% c263: 215 Bsn 1 F sharp 4 (was E4): over the bass A sharp the downbeat sounded A sharp-C sharp-E with no F sharp (the root of V6/5 of V reached only the violas on beats 2-3) and its dissonant fifth E five times (Vln I, Vln II, Cl 1, Bsn 1, Hn 2 in unison); Aldwell-Schachter PDF 243-244: VII5/3 is seldom satisfactory, V6/5 is preferred, and the tone to double is the third, not one involved in the tritone. Hn 2 keeps the seventh E4 and its fall to D sharp 4 (c093/c157), Hn 1-2 keep their sixths (c094); Bsn 1 moves E4-F sharp 4 against the violins' F sharp-E and holds F sharp into 216 (c247). Not 214: Bsn 1 F sharp there makes fifths with Cl 1/Vln II B4-C sharp 5.
% c247: 216 Bsn 1 F sharp 4 (was D sharp 4): at the half cadence the soft horn-bassoon choir doubled the leading tone at the unison (Hn 2, Bsn 1, and the violas' D sharp 4),
%   so the four winds sounded three pitches and the B chord had no fifth above the cellos. Bsn 1 now sits inside the horns' sixth D sharp 4-B4 on the fifth
%   (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 108: two bassoons and two horns in four real parts; bassoons may be set inside the horns; crossing for blend), Hn 2 alone resolves the
%   seventh E4 -> D sharp 4 (c093, c157), the Hn 1-2 sixths (c094) are unchanged; Bsn 1 leaves the unison E4 by contrary motion and falls a fourth to C sharp 4 at 217 after the breath.
% c327: 211 Hn 2 and Bsn 1 F sharp 4 for one beat, then B3 (were F sharp 4 held), and Bsn 1 B3 at 212 (was E4): the tune's new falling sixth D sharp 5-F sharp 4 (c321) landed on the
%   horn-bassoon pair's held F sharp 4 in the tune's lower octave (Cl 1, Vln II), as at 199 (Adler II PDF 273-274). D sharp 4 is not available (the violas' leading tone, c247); Bsn 1 holds B3 into 212
%   so it does not rise B-E in octaves with the bass; it rejoins E4 at 213. Hn 2 rests on 212 beat 1 as before.
% c347: 212 Bsn 1 B3 for two beats, then E4 on beat 3 over the held E bass (was B3 for the whole bar, then E4 at 213): B3-E4 at the bar line moved
%   in twelfths with the contrabassoon (E2-A2, c114) and in fifths by contrary motion with the basses (E2-A1), I to IV, the only consecutive fifths in the movement
%   outside the tuttis and the coda's designed ostinato (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 112: in a chain of chords the distribution is subordinate to correct progression of parts;
%   PDF 108: the moving part goes to the wood-wind). The E now enters while the bass stands; c327's reason (no B-E octaves with the bass at 211-212) still holds.
tCBsnOne = { \partCombineAutomatic e'2.--(\mf | e'2.-- | fis'4-- b2-- | b2-- e'4-- | e'2.-- | e'2.-- | fis'2.-- | fis'2) r4 | cis'2.--(\< | b2.-- | a2.--\f\> | fis2.-- | b2.--\mf | a2.-- | gis2.--\> | gis2)\! r4 | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | gis2(\p^\markup \italic "dolce" a4 | b2.\< | c'2.-\tweak stencil ##f \mp\> | b2-\tweak stencil ##f \p) r4 | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | }
% c087: 209-224 Bsn 2 doubles the cellos' countermelody in unison (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 76-77: 'Cellos + Fag. is among the best unison pairs, the wind strengthens the string tone and the strings predominate;
%   PDF 78-79: a low melody in the cellos doubled by a bassoon, the basses undoubled). It had duplicated the double basses' bass an octave higher, in the cellos' register,
%   crossing their line at 209, 213 and 216. The bass stays in the basses (arco) and the harp's left hand. Both bassoons mf, a degree under the cellos' f (the shared staff prints one set).
% c129: 219 Bsn 2 holds A3 (was A3-G sharp 3 with the cellos): the cellos' passing G sharp 3 no longer makes a semitone with Bsn 1's A3 on the shared bassoon staff;
%   the second sounds between different colours (cello against bassoons and violas), Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 102, 108.
tCBsnTwo = { \partCombineAutomatic b,4(\mf gis4 e4~ | e2 gis4 | fis2 b4~ | b4 gis4 fis4) | e4( cis2~ | cis2 fis4 | cis2. | fis2 b,4) \breathe | e4(\< gis2~ | gis4 e2 | a2.\f\> | fis2.) | b,2(\mf cis4 | a,4 b,2~ | b,2.~\> | b,2)\! r4 | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | }
% c088: 209-224 Hn 1-2 and Bsn 1 (the soft four-part choir of c014/c047) under phrase slurs split at the existing breath (216), tenuto on repeated notes; 233-236 Hn 3-4 likewise. The trio's first horn pad (153-168) is already tied by phrase (Adler I PDF 309, 311-312).
% c094: 218-219 Hn 1 G sharp 4 (held from 217) and A4 (were B4 and C sharp 5), so Hn 1-2 hold thirds (E-G sharp, F sharp-A) under the strings' tune instead of open fifths
%   (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 97, Remark 4: with the top part in the strings the wind's middle parts take 2nds, 7ths, 3rds or 6ths, not open 4ths and 5ths; PDF 90 rule 4); 218 now sounds the I6's third in the middle.
% c324: 209 Hn 1 G sharp 4 (was B4): it resolves the motto tail's A4 from 208 (tB), with Fl 2, Cl 1·2 and Vln II on the same G sharp; B4 from 210.
tCHnOne = { gis'2--(\p cis''4-- | b'2.-- | b'2.-- | b'2.-- | cis''2.-- | cis''2.-- | cis''2.~ | cis''4 b'2) \breathe | gis'2.--(\< | gis'2.-- | a'2.--\mf\> | cis''2.-- | b'2.--\p | b'2.-- | gis'2.--) | R2.*13 | gis'2.~\pp | gis'2.~ | gis'2.~ | gis'2 r4 | }
% c054: 212 Hn 2 enters on beat 2 with the tune's G sharp 4: the held G sharp 4 sounded under the appoggiatura A4 of Cl 1 and Vln II in the same octave
%   (Adler PDF 255: the melody must come through and not be obscured by the harmony; PDF 243: sustained winds placed apart from the theme; as c039 at 197-200).
% c129: 209 Hn 2 E4 (was G sharp 4): T's lower octave (Vln II, Cl 1) starts G sharp 4-A4 in the horn's register, so the held G sharp 4 doubled it and then rubbed against its A4
%   (the clash c039 removed at 197-200 and c047 at 229; Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 116, 132-133). Hn 1-2 open on the fifth E-B (the first statement's drone, 153) and take the third from 210.
tCHnTwo = { e'2.--(\p | e'2.-- | fis'4-- b2--) | r4 gis'2--( | a'2.-- | e'2.-- | e'2.-- | dis'2.--) \breathe | e'2.--(\< | e'2.-- | fis'2.--\mf\> | fis'2.-- | gis'2.--\p | fis'2.-- | e'2.--) | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | }
% c051: 233-236 Hn 3 E4 (was B3-A3-B3, in unison with Bsn 1's B3 at 234 and 236): the horns frame the bassoon's T head an octave apart and Bsn 1 is p,
%   a degree above the pp horns (Sebesky PDF 212: the background supplies chord tones not in the solo line; PDF 219: do not cover a soloist's weaker register).
% c126: 233-236 Hn 3 rests (was E4 pp just above Bn 1's G sharp 3-C4 statement of T's head): the bassoon in its middle register blends and is swallowed when enclosed by
%   instruments of its own range (Adler I PDF 226); Hn 4 keeps the E3 pedal below it, as the violas lie ppp under Bn 1 at 345 (c078). Hn 3 and Hn 4 re-enter pp at 237.
tCHnThree = { R2. | R2.*23 | R2.*4 | b2.~\pp | b2.~ | b2.~ | b2 r4 | }
% c317: 217-224 Hn 4 joins Vc and Bsn 2 on the countermelody for its second phrase (E3-A3-B2, written B3-E4-F#3), mp< mf> mp, a level under the cellos;
%   at 217 each element gains a colour together: Fl 1 joins T and Hn 4 the countermelody. Adler II PDF 178 (Meistersinger: each element string + woodwind + brass;
%   second theme cellos + horn at pitch), PDF 184 (Schelomo: as the climax nears, instruments are added to each element), PDF 176 (each theme at good strength).
%   Same colour as the scherzo tutti's tenor line 105-120 (Vc, Bsn 1, Hn 3-4). Hn 4 rests 209-216 after 201-208. Bass clef in the score for 217-224 (A2, c115).
tCHnFour = { R2.*8 | \tag #'score \clef bass e4(\mp\< gis2~ | gis4 e2 | a2.\mf\> | fis2.) | b,2(\mp cis4 | a,4 b,2~ | b,2.~\> | b,2)\! r4 | R2.*4 | \tag #'score \clef treble e2.--(\pp-\tag #'score ^\markup \bold "4." | e2.-- | e2.-- | e2.--) | e2.---\sysLabel "4."( | e2.-- | e2.-- | e2.--) | e2.~\pp | e2.~ | e2.~ | e2 r4 | }
tCTimp = { e,4\f r4 r4 | R2.*3 | R2.*11 | e,4\p r4 r4 | R2.^\markup \small \italic \column { "G♯ muta in G" "medium sticks" } | R2.*11 | e,2.:32\pp~ | e,2.:32~ | e,2.:32~ | e,4 r4 r4 | }
% c336: "G♯ muta in G" (was "muta G♯ in G"), Adler II PDF 312 word order ("Sol muta in Mi").
% c153: 225-236 the codetta's accompaniment thins by four-bar unit while T's head falls Ob 1 -> Cl 1 -> Bsn 1: harp off-beats on beats 2 and 3 (225-228), beat 2 only (229-232),
%   one chord with the downbeat (233-236), so the pulse goes 3 -> 2 -> 1 per bar into the poco rit. at 237 and the stretch winds down instead of standing still
%   (Adler II PDF 267-268, Mozart K. 313/iii: the accompaniment figure changes from phrase to phrase; PDF 288, Haydn: the doubling changes in the second phrase; PDF 268-269: a sparser texture
%   for the entry). Pitches, register (c047) and the iv's C natural at 235 (c042) unchanged.
% c256: "non arpegg." on the first off-beat chord of each accompaniment entry (81, 185, 225): Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 46, harp chords "must always be broken (arpeggiato); should the composer wish otherwise he should notify it (non arpeggiato)". 153 and 341 already say so; 81 said nothing, and 185 and 225 follow 16 bars rest at a new letter. Chords meant broken keep their arpeggio sign (149, 151, 237, 361).
tCHarpR = { R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2._\markup \small "A♮" | r4 <gis b e'>4_\markup \small \italic "non arpegg." <gis b e'>4 | r4 <gis b e'>4 <gis b e'>4 | r4 <a cis' e'>4 <a cis' e'>4 | r4 <gis b e'>4 <gis b e'>4 | r4 <gis b e'>4 r4 | r4 <gis b e'>4 r4 | r4 <a cis' e'>4 r4 | r4 <gis b e'>4 r4 | <e' gis' b'>4 r4 r4 | <e' gis' b'>4_\markup \small "C♮" r4 r4 | <e' a' c''>4 r4 r4 | <e' gis' b'>4 r4 r4 | <gis b e'>2.\arpeggio | R2. | R2. | \tag #'part \once \override MultiMeasureRest.minimum-length = #16 R2._\markup \small \italic "étouffez" | }  % c165: damp at the end of 240, before the harp part's page turn at 240/241 (was on 241, after the turn; MOLA PDF 7)
% c346: 214-215 C sharp 2 (was A sharp 1 with "A♯" at 213 and "A♮" at 216): the A string was re-pedalled twice in four bars while the low A1/A♯1 still rang
%   (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 46: low harp strings ring on, the harp is diatonic and does not suit rapid chromatic change). C♯ is in the chord (Vc, Bn 2, T); basses and Cbsn keep A♯.
% c354: 214-215 the harp LH rests (was C sharp 2, a minor third above the basses' A sharp 1): the harp doubles the bass note for note in 209-224, and where it cannot without re-pedalling the ringing A string (c346) it drops out rather than strike a second, higher bass note; the plucked attack is the clearest pitch in that octave (Casanelles PDF 274: thirds only from the fifth partial; PDF 275-276: attack transients).
% c364: 209-224 the harp's left hand rests (was plucked bass notes mf under the arco basses and contrabassoon on the same notes, with a two-bar gap at 214-215 for pedalling, c346/c354).
%   Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 52: plucked strings with bowed instruments do not blend, both heard independently; PDF 51: sustained groups easily overpower plucked strings;
%   PDF 53: plucked strings only at reasonable intervals; PDF 81-82 and 90 (rule 1): a doubling is kept or left out for the whole passage, not dropped for two bars mid-phrase.
%   The harp now rests 193-224 and returns with both hands at 225 for the codetta, a new idea (PDF 81). Pedal A natural moved from 209 to 224: with both hands resting, the harp staff is hidden in the system 193-213 and the mark printed at 209 was lost.
tCHarpL = { R2.*16 | e,4\pp r4 r4 | e,4 r4 r4 | a,,4 r4 r4 | e,4 r4 r4 | e,4 r4 r4 | gis,,4 r4 r4 | a,,4 r4 r4 | e,4 r4 r4 | e,4 r4 r4 | e,4 r4 r4 | a,,4 r4 r4 | e,4 r4 r4 | e,2. | R2.*3 | }
% c258: 239-240 Vln II and the violas leave the trio's last chord at 239 (were held to 240), so in the last bar of the poco rit. the horns alone hold E major
%   with Hn 1's pp G sharp on top, the pivot that turns to G at 241. Diminuendo by taking a colour away (Sebesky PDF 12-13, Ex. 1: the line that adds nothing is
%   removed so the primary element dominates; PDF 13-14: focus; PDF 217-218, Ex. 284: a drop in volume and a change of colour prepare the change), as 362-364 fade into
%   Hn 1's G sharp (c123). Printed hairpins here moved the trio's line breaks and made 15 pages; the cut does the same work. Timpani roll unchanged (c248).
% c258: 209 "espress." also on Vln II and Cl 1, which double Vln I's T at the same f (Sebesky Ex. 281, PDF 214-215: every part of a doubled line carries the
%   same marks; the parts print only their own words).
tCVlnOne = { gis''2(\f^\markup \italic "espress." a''4 | b''2 e'''4) | dis'''4.( fis''8 a''4~ | a''4\> gis''2-\tweak stencil ##f \mf) | a''2-\tweak stencil ##f \f( b''4 | cis'''4\< fis'''2-\tweak stencil ##f \ff\>) | e'''4.(-\tweak stencil ##f \f dis'''8 cis'''4 | ais''4 b''2) | cis'''2(\< dis'''4 | e'''4 gis'''4) a'''4~\ff( | a'''4.\> gis'''8 fis'''4 | cis'''4 b''4 a''4) | gis''4\f( b''4 e'''4\<~ | e'''4-\tweak stencil ##f \ff\> dis'''4-\tweak stencil ##f \f fis'''4) | e'''2.~ | e'''2\> r4\! | R2.*16 | }
tCVlnTwo = { gis'2(\f^\markup \italic "espress." a'4 | b'2 e''4) | dis''4.( fis'8 a'4~ | a'4\> gis'2-\tweak stencil ##f \mf) | a'2-\tweak stencil ##f \f( b'4 | cis''4\< fis''2-\tweak stencil ##f \ff\>) | e''4.(-\tweak stencil ##f \f dis''8 cis''4 | ais'4 b'2) | cis''2(\< dis''4 | e''4 gis''4) a''4~\ff( | a''4.\> gis''8 fis''4 | cis''4 b'4 a'4) | gis'4\f( b'4 e''4\<~ | e''4-\tweak stencil ##f \ff\> dis''4-\tweak stencil ##f \f fis''4) | e''2.~ | e''2\> r4\! | b'2.\pp | b'2. | a'2. | b'2. | e''2. | e''2. | e''2. | e''2. | b'2. | b'2. | a'2. | b'2. | b'2.~ | b'2.~ | b'2 r4 | R2. | }
% c027: 209-224 viola off-beats in the trio's first voicing (B3-E4, as at 169-184), below the tune's lower octave in Vln II and Cl 1 (were E4-B4, above it; Adler II PDF 274, 279)
% c089: 212-213 violas E4-D4 on beat 3 of the tune's fourth bar, then C sharp 4: a passing seventh, I - V7/IV - IV, so the second and third statements of the
%   trio tune are not harmonised exactly like the first (153-156 stays plain). A-S PDF 418-419 (Ex. 25-1: applied and passing applied chords in a plain theme), PDF 399 (the 7th resolves
%   down by step). The temporary leading tone G sharp is in the tune only on beat 3 (cellos have moved to F sharp, PDF 419: not doubled); F sharp (9th) -> E and D -> C sharp in 173/213.
% c157: 214-215 the third statement's second phrase rises A-A sharp-A sharp-B in the bass (double basses, contrabassoon): V6/5 of V (F sharp 7 over A sharp) for two bars
%   instead of ii - V7/V over A-F sharp-F sharp-B, so the forte return reaches the same B at 216 by another chord (Sebesky PDF 19, Ex. 3/3A: do not harmonise a melody the same way twice;
%   PDF 20: a substitution for a lift). 153-160 and 169-176 keep ii (c089: the first statement plain, later ones coloured). A sharp only in the bass (c093, A-S PDF 214); the 7th E4
%   in Bsn 1 and Hn 2, resolving to D sharp 4 at 216 (Hn 1-2 in sixths C sharp 5/E4, B4/D sharp 4, c094); violas C sharp 4/F sharp 4; cellos and Bsn 2 C sharp 3 (minim)-F sharp 3 on beat 3, after the tune has reached F sharp (no A natural, no octaves with its C sharp-F sharp);
%   harp left hand C sharp 2 (its A natural pedal stays).
% c157: 209-224 cellos' countermelody mf < f > mf with the bassoons (was f throughout, the tune's own level): Sebesky PDF 13 (the ear goes to the primary element, not to a counter-line),
%   as the second statement (169: tune mp, cellos p) and c099's one-level rule. Answers c087's 'a degree under the cellos': in the cello-bassoon unison the strings predominate anyway (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 76).
tCVla = { r4 <b e'>4--(\mf^\markup \italic "div." <a e'>4--) | r4 <b e'>4--( <b e'>4--) | r4 <a dis'>4--( <b dis'>4--) | r4 <b e'>4--( <b d'>4--) | r4 <a cis'>4--( <a cis'>4--) | r4 <cis' fis'>4--( <cis' fis'>4--) | r4 <cis' fis'>4--( <cis' fis'>4--) | r4 <b dis'>4--( <b dis'>4--) | r4 <b e'>4--(\< <b e'>4--) | r4 <b e'>4--( <b e'>4--) | r4 <cis' e'>4--(\f\> <cis' e'>4--) | r4 <fis e'>4--( <fis e'>4--) | r4 <b e'>4--(\mf <b e'>4--) | r4 <a fis'>4--( <a fis'>4--) | r4 <gis e'>4--(\> <gis e'>4--) | r4 <gis e'>4--( <gis e'>4--) | gis'2.\pp^\markup \italic "unis." | gis'2. | e'2. | gis'2. | gis2. | gis2. | a2. | gis2. | gis'2. | gis'2. | e'2. | gis'2. | gis'2.~ | gis'2.~ | gis'2 r4 | R2. | }
% c367: 225-240 the cellos rest (were pizz. E3/A2/G#2 on every downbeat, an octave over the basses and the harp's left hand, with Hn 4's held E3 from 229): Sebesky PDF 140 rule 7 (the root is the least important note; the lowest cello doubling the bass out of habit 'grounds' the sound), PDF 145-146 (Ex. 195; the bottom cello plays roots for richness, not to reinforce the bass), PDF 157 and 173 (Ex. 204 ends on the bass alone; cellos tacet so as not to clutter the solo). The pedal stays in Cb, harp and Hn 4, as at 153-168 and 341-348 (c267); Bsn 1's T head at 233-236 (G#3-C4) no longer sits a third over a plucked E3. Pizz. now at 241.
tCVc = { b,4(\mf gis4 e4~ | e2 gis4) | fis2( b4~ | b4 gis4 fis4) | e4( cis2~ | cis2 fis4) | cis2.( | fis2) b,4 | e4(\< gis2~ | gis4 e2) | a2(\f\> gis4 | fis2.) | b,2(\mf cis4 | a,4) b,2 | b,2.~\upbow\> | b,2 r4\! | R2.*16 | }
% c033: 209-224 double basses arco, sustaining the bass under T's forte return (pizzicato at 153 and 169): the third statement is scored differently
%   by alteration of detail (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 118 d); the harp's left hand keeps the plucked downbeat; pizz. again at 225 after a beat's rest;
%   216 B-F sharp (fifth of V on beat 3), so the sustained bass does not move B-C sharp in octaves with T's B5-C sharp 6 into 217.
% c101: 209-224 the contrabassoon doubles the basses' bass line in unison, a degree softer: in T's forte return the cellos and Bsn 2 carry the countermelody,
%   so the bass was in the basses alone (with the harp's plucked downbeats). Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 56: no phrase of importance in the double basses without the
%   support of cellos or bassoons; Ex. 11 (Golden Cockerel), double basses + double bassoon; PDF 54: the double bass is dull in tone. Unison, so nothing crosses the cellos (c094).
% c114: 213-222 the contrabassoon doubles the basses an octave higher (sounding A2-F#2-C#3-G#2-B2), back in unison on E at 223: the bass fell to F#1/A1
%   with nothing up to A3/C#3 (219: F#1-A3, 27 semitones); Rimsky-Korsakov appendix PDF 505-507 (the bass always has its octave) and PDF 125. Under the cellos' line, no crossing (c087).
% c184: 221-222 contrabassoon B1 with the basses (was B2): at B2 it sat inside the cello/Bsn 2 countermelody, which crossed below it, so A2-B2 and B2-C sharp 3 seconds sounded at the bottom of the chord
%   (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 82 note: the bass never mixes with the other parts; PDF 85 and 96 remark 2: wide intervals at the bottom). The octave above the bass is still sounded by the countermelody's B2 (221 b1-2, 222 b2-3); c114's octave stays at 213-220.
% c384 (Rimsky-Korsakov appendix PDF 505-507, ch. IV PDF 125): 210-212 the contrabassoon takes the octave above the basses (G sharp 2, B2, E3), as c114 does at 213-220:
%   since c382 the bass falls to G sharp 1 at 210, and since c364 the harp no longer doubles it (c294 kept the unison for the harp), so the bass stood alone in octave 1
%   with nothing up to the cellos' E3/F sharp 3/B3 (20, 19, 19 semitones). 209 stays in unison (E2-C sharp 2): the cellos' B2 on its downbeat would sit under an E3.
tCCbsn = { e,2--(\mp cis,4-- | gis,2.-- | b,2.-- | e2.--) | a,2.--( | ais,2.-- | ais,2.-- | b,2 fis,4) \breathe | cis2.--(\< | gis,2.-- | fis,2.--\mf\> | a,2.-- | b,,2.--\mp | b,,2.-- | e,2.~\> | e,2)\! r4 | R2.*16 | }
% c382: 209 beat 3 IV6 under T's 4th note A (bass E2-C sharp 2-G sharp 1, I - IV6 - I6, the bass falling a sixth under 3-4-5; Aldwell-Schachter PDF 199-200, Ex. 13-7 Haydn 101/iv and 13-8, 13-9a):
%   Cb and Cbsn C sharp 2 then G sharp 1; Vc and Bsn 2 G sharp 3-E3 (tied into 210, the 3rd above the bass, PDF 169); violas A3-E4; Hn 1 G sharp 4-C sharp 5-B4. 153 (plain) and 169 (IV over the cellos' A) unchanged.
tCCb = { e,2\mf^\markup \italic "arco" cis,4 | gis,,2. | b,,2. | e,2. | a,,2. | ais,,2. | ais,,2. | b,,2 fis,,4 | cis,2.(\< | gis,,2.) | fis,,2.(\f\> | a,,2.) | b,,2.\mf | b,,2. | e,2.~\upbow\> | e,2 r4\! | e,4\pp^\markup \italic "pizz." r4 r4 | e,4 r4 r4 | a,,4 r4 r4 | e,4 r4 r4 | e,4 r4 r4 | gis,,4 r4 r4 | a,,4 r4 r4 | e,4 r4 r4 | e,4 r4 r4 | e,4 r4 r4 | a,,4 r4 r4 | e,4 r4 r4 | e,4 r4 r4 | e,4 r4 r4 | e,4 r4 r4 | e,4 r4 r4 | }
% c047: 211 and 222 Hn 1 holds B (concord) instead of the V7's seventh A (A4 rose to B4 at 211-212; at 222 it doubled Bsn 1's A3): Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 108,
%   horns + bassoons in soft four-part harmony, concords to the horns, discords to the bassoons; stationary parts to the brass.
% c047: 229-232 Vln II E5 and violas B3-A3 frame Cl 1's T head (it began in unison with the violas' held G sharp 4, its A4 rubbed against it, and its B4 sat on Vln II's held B4,
%   as at 197-200 before c039); 233-236 harp off-beats E4-G sharp 4-B4 (E4-A4-C5 at 235), above Bsn 1's T head G sharp 3-C4 inside Hn 3-4 (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 108, 112).
% c045: 224 timpani E p (it still read f from 209, a loud stroke in the diminuendo into the pp codetta). 229 Cl 2 p with Cl 1's p entry: the shared staff prints one dynamic.
% c067: 209 Fl 1 (and Fl 2's G sharp, same staff) mf under the violins' f: a flute on the lead violin line is marked a degree softer (Sebesky PDF 164).
% c081: T bars 10-11 (162, 178, 218, 350): the climax A is anticipated on beat 3 of bar 10 and tied over the bar line (was e4 gis2 | a4. gis8 fis4, now e4 gis4 a4~ | a4. gis8 fis4).
%   The phrase's highest note becomes its longest and a syncopation, the only bar-line tie before the cadence (Aldwell-Schachter Unit 3 PDF 50: long and high notes take accent;
%   PDF 51: a note begun on a weak beat and held through the strong beat contradicts the metre; PDF 56, Ex. 3-19: the anticipation held over into the consonance, here I6 -> ii).
%   Dvorak 9/iii's E-major tune (Ob 1, 68-76) ties over five bar lines in nine bars. Theme change (third phrase only); violins re-bow on the A.
% c112: 219-220 violas hold E4 over ii7-ii6/5 into the cadential 6/4 at 221 (7th prepared at 218 and held in the voice that becomes the 4th; A-S PDF 177, 181-182, Ex. 12-8); removes the 5ths Hn 1 C#5-B4 / Vla F#4-E4 at 220-221.
% c174: 214-215 harp LH A sharp 1 with the basses (pedal A sharp after 213, A natural after 216), not C sharp 2 a minor third above them; 223-224 Vc and Bsn 2 hold B2, the fifth over E2, instead of G sharp 2, a major third over the bass with no fifth in the chord (Casanelles PDF 264, 274: harmonic series)
% c295: 209 Cl 2's beat-3 rest at staff-position -6 (score only): the default sat 3.5 spaces below the centre, half a space off the lines (Gould, Behind Bars PDF 49: rests move an exact number of stave-spaces; PDF 50: lower-part rest below the centre).
% c343: 230 the pizz. bass and harp LH take G sharp (Vc G#2, Cb and harp G#1) under Cl 1's B4: the codetta's second group becomes I-I6-IV-I (bass E-G#-A-E) instead of a third
%   I-I-IV-I. The bass falls a sixth to the third of the chord and rises by step to IV, in compound tenths with the tune's B-C#; the first group stays on the root and the third keeps its iv.
%   Aldwell-Schachter PDF 107 (Ex. 7-2: moving the bass between root and third 'makes it possible to continue the same harmony without monotony'), PDF 108 (Ex. 7-4a: parallel tenths),
%   PDF 110 (Ex. 7-8b: the bass may reach I6 by a falling sixth), PDF 108 sec. 4 (any tone of I6 may be doubled). Harmonic plan IV IV iv (c173) and the hand-overs Ob-Cl-Bsn kept.
